$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\dfrac{x-4}{3-\sqrt{x+5}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{x-4}{3-\sqrt{x+5}}\cdot\dfrac{3+\sqrt{x+5}}{3+\sqrt{x+5}}\qquad\gray{\text{Rationalize the denominator}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{(x-4)(3+\sqrt{x+5})}{3^2-(x+5)}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{(x-4)(3+\sqrt{x+5})}{-x+4}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\cancel{(x-4)}(3+\sqrt{x+5})}{-1\cancel{(x-4)}}\qquad\gray{\text{Cancel out common factors}}
\\\\
&={-3-\sqrt{x+5}} \text{, for }x\neq 4
\end{align}$

This means that the two expressions have the same value for all $x$-values (in their domains) except for $4$.

We can now use the following theorem:

If $f(x)=g(x)$ for all $x$-values in a given interval except for $x=c$, then $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to c}f(x)=\lim_{x\to c}g(x)$.
